id: pm-jt8aa2
title: "A budget-truncated read reports that rows are missing without a cursor, a recovery, or any sign that it overrode the caller's explicit request for all of them"
description: "compactRowsToBudget halves the largest row collection until the estimate fits the ceiling, then sets has_more and truncated and recomputes count. It writes nothing else. The result is an envelope that states rows are missing and provides no way to obtain them.\n\nMeasured at HEAD build 2026.8.13 against this tracker of 2,473 items:\n\n  pm list --status open                 84 of 339 rows, has_more true, next_cursor null\n  pm list --status open --no-truncate   84 of 339 rows, has_more true, next_cursor null\n\nThe second line is the defect that matters. --no-truncate shipped under pm-0c0j as an explicit override of any row limit, and the receipt proves the layer saw it: legacy_aliases_used records --no-truncate and migration_hints records 'prefer --output-limit unbounded'. The request is resolved, recorded, and then discarded by a ceiling whose own source field reads 'default'. The declared precedence is canonical, legacy, intent, default, so the weakest layer silently overrides the strongest, and nothing in the envelope says so.\n\nRecovery is unreachable from the response. next_cursor is null because the row limit was never hit, so --after has nothing to continue from. The only spelling that returns the complete result, --output-budget unbounded, appears nowhere in the output. The whole-result omission path does carry restore_with; the row-compaction path, which is the common case, carries none, so the surface teaches recovery exactly when it does not need to and never when it does.\n\nThe consequence at scale is a wrong answer rather than a partial one. An agent asking for the open backlog receives 84 rows, an assertion that more exist, and no next step. It cannot tell that 255 items were withheld, and every count it derives is wrong by construction. This is the fifth consequence of the same inversion pm-5t33or names: a declared ceiling became binding without the envelope disclosure that criterion required."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
