comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-13T21:19:00.373Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence: shipped. attachReadOutputTruncationDisclosure in src/sdk/read-output-contracts.ts attaches output_budget_truncation whenever the receipt records rows_compacted, at the point where the binding budget and the resolved dimensions are both in scope. It carries the reason, the budget source and value, the list of explicitly requested dimensions the budget overrode, and a restore_with naming both the complete-result spelling and the paging alternative.\n\nVerified live: 'pm list --status open --no-truncate' now returns count 84, total 339, truncated true, has_more true, and output_budget_truncation with budget_source default, budget_tokens 6000, overridden_dimensions [\"amount\"], restore_with naming --output-budget unbounded. Before the change the same invocation returned the same 84 rows with next_cursor null and no statement that the explicitly requested unbounded row read had been overridden by a default-sourced ceiling.\n\nThe overridden_dimensions field is the part that matters: it is computed from the resolved dimension set, so it reports precedence inversion as data rather than as prose, and it is empty when the caller made no explicit request. Full suite green, 7,272 tests."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-13T21:19:12.108Z","harness:claude-code","A precedence table is a claim that must be enforced somewhere, and 'default' is the layer most likely to break it, because a default is applied by a different code path than the one that resolves explicit requests. Here the resolution layer recorded the caller's explicit unbounded row request, printed a migration hint about it, and then let a default-sourced token ceiling override it silently — the receipt contained every fact needed to notice, and nothing compared them. When a resolved dimension set and a binding bound are both in scope, the comparison is one filter, and its output is the disclosure."
